WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.450
We'll continue.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.720
And let me remind you where we
are, what the big picture is.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.480
The big picture is there are
some times when someone says,

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.460
is this convex or this concave.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.040
Sometimes you actually just
go back to the definition

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.680
or calculate a Hessian
or something like that.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.650
That's actually
really very rare.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.608
Really, the way you're
going to be doing this--

00:00:28.608 --> 00:00:30.317
and a lot of people
do this in practice--

00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:31.520
is in a constructive way.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.660
And so that's basically
a calculus, as I said.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.650
You learn a bunch of
base convex functions,

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.050
a bunch of base convex sets.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.390
And then you learn
the rules that you

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.450
can use to combine them and
to know that the combination--

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.780
or to transform them and to
know that transformation is also

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:50.380
convex.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.270
And so that then,
in fact, that's

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.400
going to be also how
all the code works

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.300
as well, as we'll see.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.192
So OK.

00:00:58.192 --> 00:00:59.400
So we're going through these.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.390
I think last time I
mentioned some of these

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.700
are simple and obvious.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.940
Pretty soon, though, they're
not simple or obvious,

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.120
some of these rules.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.870
So the first are--

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.660
look, if something is convex,
then 3.7 times it is convex.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.620
Well, sure, right.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.400
If something curves upward and
then you multiply it by 3.7,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.140
it curves more upward.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.690
So that's fine.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.060
The sum of two convex
functions is convex.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.727
And, again, some of
these things-- and I

00:01:28.727 --> 00:01:31.185
would actually encourage you
to do this every now and then.

00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:32.580
You just grab one
of these things

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.340
and just prove-- some of these
are like three-line things

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.150
to show.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.600
But somebody has to show them.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.880
I'm not going to do it.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.760
So the sum.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.420
Precomposition with an affine
function-- precomposition

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:51.240
refers to the fact that the
affine function appears first.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.610
So first, I apply
an affine function.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.580
I map x to Ax plus b.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.410
And then, I take this
convex function f.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.450
And that preserves convexity.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.270
And, again, this is not-- these
are not-- that's a three liner.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.760
And so these are
the kind of things

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:10.360
you might want to
just show yourself.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.170
OK, so these are
relatively simple.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.930
Then we get to the
so-called pointwise maximum.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:23.500
So the maximum of a bunch of
functions is defined pointwise.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.100
And just to convince you that
this would preserve convexity--

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.753
I mean, I'll just
draw a picture.

00:02:28.753 --> 00:02:30.670
I mean, it's not too
hard to actually show it.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.910
But if I have one
function here and I

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.740
have another one over
here that's convex,

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.172
there's another one, then if
I look at the maximum, that's

00:02:40.172 --> 00:02:42.130
going to-- the pointwise
maximum is going to be

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.190
this function here, like that.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.630
So that's what the--
and it's pretty clear.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.290
At least your eye tells
you that's convex.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.070
And it's not hard to show this.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.200
So the maximum of a bunch of
convex functions is convex.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.010
That's a basic thing.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.110
It's kind of obvious.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.780
So here's a so-called
piecewise linear function.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.590
One way to describe a piecewise
linear function-- by the way,

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.950
the canonical way is
to say, oh, well, here

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.600
is actually have a Voronoi
decomposition of Rn.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.580
And then in each
one, just specify

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.550
the affine-- in each region,
you specify the affine function.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.560
That's the typical way.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.458
Actually, for convex
piecewise linear functions,

00:03:27.458 --> 00:03:29.750
there's actually a lot--
there are many other ways that

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.060
are better to represent it.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.990
One is simply the max of
a set of affine functions.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:37.270
So there, I give you a
bunch of affine functions.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:39.040
And then I simply
take the maximum.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.680
And that function is
going to be convex.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.730
That's a convex function.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:48.140
Here's a very interesting
one is the sum--

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.200
so here's a function on Rn.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.920
The sum of let's say the
four biggest entries.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:54.940
Everybody got that?

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.985
So it's a weird function.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.560
In fact, you can--

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.010
I mean, it's not differentiable.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:11.560
The derivative doesn't exist
at some points, actually.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.030
Let's see.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.730
It certainly doesn't
have a Hessian.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.070
It turns out this
function is convex.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.190
And the way to show
that is actually

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.040
pretty straightforward.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.580
What it is is it's the maximum
of a bunch of functions.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.430
This function here,
it's all possible sets--

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.490
let's do the sum of the
four largest entries.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.570
It is all possible
subsets choices

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.240
of four entries in the vector.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.560
And I sum those up.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.810
And then I take the maximum.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.810
Now, how many are there?

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:44.860
N choose 4.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.290
If n is big, that's
actually a very big number.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:48.880
Doesn't matter.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.380
So this is convex.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.250
Everybody, this makes sense?

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.250
So this is the sum
of the-- so people

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.160
refer to this as the sum of the
R largest or the sum of the k

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:57.940
largest.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.490
And it's a convex function.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.280
That's not totally
obvious, I think.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.800
By the way, you can extend this
to the sum of the 5.6 largest

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.750
entries of a vector.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.910
What would that mean?

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.555
What's the sum of the largest
5.6 entries of a vector?

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.960
Weighted average.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.670
Well, weighted
average, yeah, kind of.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.980
But let's be more specific.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:25.030
I give you a vector.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.120
And I say, what's the sum
of the 5.6 largest entries?

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.630
You tell me what to do.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:34.510
Is it just the sum of
the five largest entries?

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.200
It's the sum of the
five largest entries

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.650
plus 0.6 times
the sixth largest.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:41.820
Everybody got that?

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:42.360
So yeah.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.980
When you said take a
weighted combination, sure.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:44.772
But that was vague.

00:05:44.772 --> 00:05:46.610
And I wanted something
more specific.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:49.020
So everybody got that?

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.910
That's a weird function.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:51.750
Guess what?

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.400
It's convex.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.130
And you can-- it's
the same thing,

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:01.710
that you simply look at all
possible combinations of sums

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.480
of-- actually, you
could do in this case

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:08.490
six things with one of
them having a weight of 0.6

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.440
and the others
having a weight of 1.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.133
And you just sum
over all of those.

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:13.050
There's a lot of them.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:13.675
Doesn't matter.

00:06:13.675 --> 00:06:15.210
Each one of those is linear.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.130
And the maximum of a
bunch of linear functions

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:18.690
is going to be convex.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.230
So OK.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:27.390
Now, this actually
generalizes to a supremum.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.990
So supremum is, if you've had
analysis, you know what it is.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.390
If you haven't, it's
basically the maximum

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:36.930
of an infinite
number of numbers.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:38.580
And there are some subtleties.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.300
It could be infinity, for
example, which is not possible

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.880
if I have a maximum of a
finite number of numbers.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:45.810
So that's what the--

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.120
so if you see sup, you can
just replace this with max.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.670
And you will be just fine.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:56.520
OK, so suppose I have a function
of two variables, x and y.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:59.920
By the way, here, x is a vector.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.920
It turns out y doesn't
even have to be a vector.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.480
It could be some weird thing.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.180
It could actually be some
combinatorial structure

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.850
like a path and a graph
or something like that.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.170
It doesn't make any difference.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.100
It's anything at all.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.980
And so you might
even think of this

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.180
as a family of convex
functions parametrized by y.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.310
That would be a good
way to think of this.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.110
And it says that
if you simply take

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:28.420
the maximum over all of
these values of y here,

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:29.950
that gives you a function.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:31.450
And it's convex.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.565
And this is also not
too hard to show.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.380
OK, so that's-- so we can
look at some examples here.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.540
Here's one.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.850
Let's see, Oh, this is the
support function of a set.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:52.580
So if I have a set here, then
the maximum over the set of y

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:59.690
transpose x that is a function
of x, that is actually convex.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.610
By the way, there
are no restrictions

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:05.240
whatsoever on the set C. Doesn't
have to be convex or anything.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:05.960
Yeah.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.080
When it says y is an element
of A, what does that A mean?

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:14.130
Script A is actually any
set at all, anything at all.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:15.630
That was my point
before when I said

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.060
it doesn't have to be a vector.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.540
Sometimes it is a vector.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:19.830
But it doesn't have to be.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.788
It could be some weird
combinatorial structure.

00:08:21.788 --> 00:08:24.920
It could be-- literally,
it's the maximum

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.560
over all paths in a
graph that would take you

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:28.400
from here to here.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:29.960
It doesn't matter.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:34.049
So it could be anything at all,
permutations, stuff like that.

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:41.870
OK, here's one is let's take a
set C, not necessarily convex.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:47.930
And we work-- and we work out
here the farthest distance

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:49.970
to the set.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.360
So what is the distance to the
farthest point in that set?

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.250
The usual distance
is the smallest is--

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.750
so if someone-- the universal
definition of the distance

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:01.260
of a point to a set--

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:02.570
so let's see.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:03.200
Here's a set.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.190
The universal definition of
distance, if this is X and this

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:11.000
is S--

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.680
so distance of X, S, it's
the minimum distance.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.200
So it would be
something like that.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.300
This is the farthest distance.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:21.710
And so that would be--

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:23.870
I have no idea what it would be.

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:24.370
Who knows?

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.912
It's either this
point or that point.

00:09:25.912 --> 00:09:28.140
It's something like either
that, something like that.

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.960
Or maybe something
way over here.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:31.580
I'm not sure.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:35.030
Anyway, that farthest
distance is convex.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.900
And by the way,
it's not too hard

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.420
for you to start
imagining applications

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.610
where things like
this would come up.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.590
So this could be--

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:52.620
this could be of a
set of where you want

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.080
to provide wireless coverage.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.015
X is a base station or something
like that that you're placing.

00:09:59.015 --> 00:10:01.140
And then this becomes very
interesting because it's

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.290
the farthest distance from
your base station to a place

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.950
that you're contractually
required to cover or something

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:07.450
like that.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.690
So we're starting to get close
to things that are clearly

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.170
useful in practice.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:15.660
So we'll get to that.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.870
OK, here's a good one,
the maximum eigenvalue

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.250
of a symmetric matrix.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.490
So now, the maximum
eigenvalue of a matrix, that's

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.380
a really complicated function.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.150
Basically, you form
the determinant

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:31.410
of lambda I minus A. That's
an nth degree polynomial.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.420
You find the roots
of the polynomial.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.190
In this case, they're all real.

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.300
And then you take the
largest of those roots.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:40.510
And you might know that if
the polynomial has degree

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:44.290
5 or larger, there's no formula
for the roots of a polynomial

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.330
like there is for
quadratic that you know.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.640
Some of you might have
seen it for a cubic.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:49.810
Anybody?

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:51.880
I'm hoping they don't
teach that anymore.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.120
I'm super happy to hear
that no one knows that.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.670
And for a quartic--

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:00.640
part of the reason they don't
teach it is because the--

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:01.990
you know the quadratic formula.

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.250
That's totally fine.

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:05.320
Cubic gets a paragraph.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.580
Quartic is a page and a half.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:08.632
And it's not cool.

00:11:08.632 --> 00:11:10.090
And then, of course,
mathematicians

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:12.340
sat around and said, well,
what is it for the quintic?

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:14.440
And then, essentially,
it was shown--

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:16.140
I don't know-- 200 years is--

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.250
yes, coming up on 200 years ago.

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:20.080
It was actually just
about 200 years ago,

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:22.060
I think, it was shown
that there's actually

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:23.260
no formula for the quintic.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:24.102
So they could stop.

00:11:24.102 --> 00:11:26.560
Now, of course, that doesn't
stop anybody who actually does

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:29.540
stuff, because people
find eigenvalues

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.930
and solve and find roots
of polynomials every day,

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:33.670
all day long.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.380
But they do it numerically.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.600
But there's no secret
formula for it.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.360
OK, all right, that was
a long, weird thing.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:44.060
OK, all I'm saying
is the function,

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:46.960
which is the largest
eigenvalue of, let's say,

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.910
a 10 by 10 symmetric
matrix, it's

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:51.070
a really complicated function.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:52.000
And I can't write it.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.980
There's no formula for
it and blah, blah, blah.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:55.630
But it's convex.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:58.090
And the way to show
it is really simple.

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:02.740
What kind of function
is y transpose Xy of x?

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:06.960
It's linear.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.720
So this shows you, you really
have to be on your toes.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.580
If I write that thing out--

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:14.280
so if I write out--

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:20.250
what is it-- y transpose
Xy, that's the sum of i

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:27.510
and j of yi, yj times Xij.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.920
Now, this function
is quadratic in y.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:34.480
But it is linear in x.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:36.235
So that's a-- and
that's actually

00:12:36.235 --> 00:12:38.360
going to be sort of a theme
is that a lot of times,

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:42.440
you just really have
to be on your toes,

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.602
reminding yourself what's
the variable, what's not,

00:12:44.602 --> 00:12:45.560
all that kind of stuff.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:49.250
And things that don't look
linear actually sometimes are.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:50.690
We'll get to examples of that.

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:52.340
OK, so here it is.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:57.470
This thing, y transpose Xy
is linear and, therefore,

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:00.080
convex for any value of y.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.560
It's a linear function
of capital X. But now,

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.470
this says if I take the supremum
over everything on the unit

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:07.250
sphere--

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:10.160
so y is this sphere--

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.193
then this thing, it
just turns out to be--

00:13:13.193 --> 00:13:14.360
first of all, that's convex.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.210
But it also is the
maximum eigenvalue.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.920
So does this make sense?

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.370
So it's going to get--

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:25.340
things are going to slowly get
a little bit more complicated

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:26.960
and maybe less obvious.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:31.250
OK, now, we're going
to do composition.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.110
This is actually
super important.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:35.990
You'll know why in about
5 minutes, 10 minutes.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:37.970
I'll make a big deal
out of it because this

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:39.710
is going to be
actually a huge part

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:41.660
of your life for the next--

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:43.640
well, certainly for
the next eight weeks,

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:47.750
it will be and quite
likely long after that.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:49.650
So we'll just start simple.

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.480
It goes like this.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:55.920
When is the composition
of two functions convex?

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:00.870
So when is h of g of x convex?

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:02.640
Oh, we know one-- by
the way, we already

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.910
know one version of composition.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:11.333
If g is affine and h
is convex, this thing

00:14:11.333 --> 00:14:12.750
is-- because that's
precomposition

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:13.770
with an affine function.

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.510
That's convex.

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:17.790
We're going to go
beyond that now.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.280
We're going to have
g be not affine.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:23.430
OK, so here's the rule.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:29.160
It says that this composition
is convex if g is convex,

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:30.600
h is convex.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.010
And I'm just going to be--

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:33.840
I'm going to be
very just informal

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:34.890
and just say increasing.

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:40.890
So an increasing convex function
of a convex function is convex.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:44.640
Now, the h tilde means it
has to be non-decreasing,

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:46.350
this extended value extension.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:50.070
So that's a-- and
that's not a weird--

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.035
it's actually a real thing.

00:14:52.035 --> 00:14:55.320
But the rough way to think
of it is increasing function

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:56.640
of a convex function is convex.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.140
Most famous example,
if g is convex,

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:02.850
then exp g of x is convex.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:06.870
So e to the x squared is convex.

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:07.610
Period.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:08.110
Why?

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:13.110
Because exp is convex
and increasing.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:18.930
And what this says is convex
increasing of a convex function

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:21.570
is convex.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.490
Here's one.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:25.530
It also works this way.

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:30.600
If h is decreasing, if the
outer function is decreasing,

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.880
if it's convex and--

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:39.340
if it's convex and decreasing,
then if g is concave,

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:43.215
this composition is convex.

00:15:43.215 --> 00:15:45.340
And by the way, you can
get that from the other one

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:48.040
by substituting a minus
with g and stuff like that.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:49.970
So that would do that.

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:53.080
So here's an example,
1 over g of x.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:59.800
The inverse of a positive
concave function is convex.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:02.620
OK, so I mean, you
could check some things.

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:05.740
Let's say 1 over square root x.

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:08.020
Square root x is--

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:11.650
well, let's limit it
to-- it doesn't matter.

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:13.070
It's non-negative and so on.

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:17.110
So 1 over that is
actually convex.

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:20.080
And the reason is
the function 1 over x

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:25.300
is convex and decreasing.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:26.470
So it looks like this.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:27.610
That's 1 over x.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.290
It's both convex and--

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:31.960
so actually, please note
that all of these arguments

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:38.660
involve basically the signs
of the 0-th, first, and second

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:42.410
derivative because I'm talking
increasing and decreasing

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:44.240
is the sign of the
first derivative

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:47.090
if it's differentiable.

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:49.010
Convexity and concavity,
that's the sine

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:51.440
of the second derivative,
again, if it's differentiable.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:53.690
But this is not a bad
way to think of it.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.150
And then we also sometimes
say, well, something

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:59.220
has to be-- well, OK,
here, g had to be positive.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:02.990
Otherwise, 1 over x, at least in
this class, doesn't make sense.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:09.780
OK, so these are the
composition rules.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:12.300
And lots of ways-- it's
actually very short

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.755
to just prove it directly
and with no assumptions

00:17:15.755 --> 00:17:17.130
on differentiability
or anything.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:18.880
But I think a very
good way to think of it

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:20.730
the way I think of it
or my mnemonic for it

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.890
if I forget this--

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:24.010
I guess I don't forget it.

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:26.280
But if I were to forget
it, I would simply

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.680
go back to something like this.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:32.280
You just assume
everything is in R1.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:33.630
Sorry, it's in R1.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.140
Assume everything
is differentiable.

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:38.640
And then you literally just go
back to your calculus class.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:39.480
Differentiate this.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:42.450
Once you get h prime of g
of x times g prime of x,

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:45.760
differentiate it a second time,
and you get this thing here.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:47.700
And now, we look
at that formula.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.170
And you take a look at it.

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:51.690
And let's do the second one.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.210
How about this one?

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:55.680
And by the way,
these assumptions

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:58.890
are going to tell us about
the signs of derivatives

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:00.540
of order 0, 1, or 2.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.050
So let's see how this works.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.350
So let's say-- let's look
at this one. g is concave.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.360
Well, let's see what that means.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:10.090
Oh, that means that's
a negative number.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:10.900
I'm going to be--

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:12.715
I'm going to be a
little informal.

00:18:12.715 --> 00:18:14.590
I'm not going to
distinguish between positive

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.360
and non-negative
and all that stuff.

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:18.880
So this is negative.

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:20.070
Let's look at this.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:21.730
So let's see h prime of g of x.

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:23.770
Well, let's see what--

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:27.400
h is decreasing.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:30.670
So that means h prime of g
of x, that's also less than

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:31.600
or equal to 0.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.390
And now, we're going to use a
very deep mathematical fact.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:35.140
Are you ready?

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.150
The product of two
numbers each of which

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:40.540
is less than or equal to 0
is bigger than or equal to 0.

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:41.350
Everybody got that?

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:43.930
Extremely deep
mathematical principle.

00:18:43.930 --> 00:18:47.800
So literally, we're using
the dumbest sign arithmetic

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:49.310
for multiplication.

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:52.210
So this term with
these assumptions here,

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.980
this term bigger
than or equal to 0.

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:56.740
Now, let's look at this term.

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:59.390
Well, g prime of x squared--

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:02.800
well, any square of anything
is going to be non-negative.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:07.470
And then we multiply that
by h prime prime of g of x.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.120
But we're assuming h is convex.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.550
That means that h prime
prime is non-negative.

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:12.490
Everybody got it?

00:19:12.490 --> 00:19:15.000
So once again, deep
mathematical principle.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.280
The product of a
non-negative number

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.740
times the square of
another number, that's

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:20.980
going to be non-negative.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:23.850
And then we're going to use
an even more basic fact, which

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.100
is the sum of two non-negative
things is non-negative.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.010
Everybody got that?

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:29.070
So all of this is non-negative.

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:31.080
Oh, but that's f prime prime.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:34.890
That's exactly what it
means for f to be convex.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:36.143
Did everybody follow that?

00:19:36.143 --> 00:19:37.560
I mean, it's kind
of a dumb thing.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:38.830
But I wanted to go through it.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:39.780
It doesn't hurt
to go through it.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:40.620
Yes.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.367
Is this just in one direction?

00:19:42.367 --> 00:19:43.950
Or does it hold the
other way as well?

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:45.400
Or other examples--

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:45.900
It is.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:47.560
It does not hold the other way.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:49.380
So that's a very good question.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:53.020
If this composition rule--

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:55.500
if the hypothesis of the
composition rule holds,

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.770
you can conclude
something is convex.

00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.520
I mean, there's a
similar one for concave.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:08.090
You can have cases where the
composition rule does not hold.

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:11.200
And yet, the function is convex.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.540
So, that's an example.

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:16.030
We'll see many examples of that.

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:21.070
So, OK, everybody got this?

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:23.500
OK, and here's the main one.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:25.870
And actually, there's only
just one variation on this.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:28.480
And then it's the one that
will be a very important part

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:29.200
of your life.

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:31.550
So here it is.

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:33.730
It says, now, what
I'm going to do

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:35.720
is I'm going to have h
has multiple arguments.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:42.520
So h is a convex function
on a vector arguments.

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:45.040
And g will have,
let's say, k entries.

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:47.900
g of x, I'm writing
for the arguments.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:50.560
These are the entries
of g1, g2, up to gx.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:52.720
And so here's your composition.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:57.430
And let's see what--
the rule is this.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:01.060
It says that f is convex,
provided the outer calling

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.420
function is convex, and--

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:06.738
then there's two
statements here;

00:21:06.738 --> 00:21:09.280
although, in fact, the stronger
statements, you can mix them.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:10.630
But here it is.

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:11.600
Here's one case.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:16.990
It says that h is increasing
in all the arguments,

00:21:16.990 --> 00:21:20.220
and these are all convex.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:23.280
Here's another one.
h is decreasing

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:26.220
in all of the arguments,
and these are all concave.

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:30.690
Oh, by the way, here's
another one to round it out.

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:34.380
No conditions on monotonicity
on h, but all of the arguments

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:35.610
are affine.

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:37.140
Everybody got that?

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:42.272
OK, so now, it's time for me
to tell you the full story.

00:21:42.272 --> 00:21:43.980
I think we have a
homework problem on it.

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.438
I mean, it should honestly have
been a problem in the book,

00:21:46.438 --> 00:21:47.400
but too bad.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:48.960
Then it's this.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:54.360
The most general form says, this
function is convex provided--

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:56.723
now, I'll say it--
you're going to see this.

00:21:56.723 --> 00:21:58.890
You're going to live and
breathe it for that matter.

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:00.182
But it's going to go like this.

00:22:00.182 --> 00:22:03.240
It says, provided
h is convex and--

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.710
now, I'm going to iterate
over the arguments--

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:10.340
for each argument,
either A, the argument

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.820
is affine in which case there's
no monotonicity requirement

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.520
on h at all.

00:22:16.520 --> 00:22:20.680
B, that argument
is convex, and h

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.780
is increasing in that argument.

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:26.830
Or C, the argument
is concave, and h

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:28.908
is decreasing in that argument.

00:22:28.908 --> 00:22:31.450
So it just makes it-- you can
just freely mix and match these

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:32.020
things--

00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:34.810
affine, convex, concave,
and monotonicity.

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:36.520
Everybody got it?

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:38.650
So actually, here's
what's weird.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:42.880
That one rule subsumes
basically all the others.

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:47.710
Let's give a really weird,
pedantic, long proof

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:52.060
that the sum of k convex
functions is convex.

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:53.680
Now, first of all,
that's obvious.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:55.323
But let's prove it using this.

00:22:55.323 --> 00:22:57.490
So I'm going to show that
this actually subsumes all

00:22:57.490 --> 00:22:59.050
the rules you've seen so far.

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:00.460
So let's do that.

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:03.907
So here's my-- the
way I'm going to do

00:23:03.907 --> 00:23:05.740
that is I'm going to
make up a new function.

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:10.540
It's going to be-- so h
of, let's say, z1 up to zk

00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:11.690
is going to be this.

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:13.180
It's going to be z1.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.430
It's going to-- I'm
going to add them up.

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:17.620
That's my function.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:21.280
Somebody tell me about
curvature of that function.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:22.030
Is it convex?

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:22.850
Is it concave?

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:23.350
It's both.

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:24.370
It's both.

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:26.410
OK, but I'm doing convex.

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:28.330
So it's convex.

00:23:28.330 --> 00:23:30.730
Now, tell me about
monotonicity of this function

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:33.160
in its arguments.

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:38.320
That means if I increase x1,
does that function go up? z1,

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:40.660
does that function
go up or down?

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:41.530
Up.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:44.380
OK, so it's monotone
in all the arguments.

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.495
It's monotone increasing.

00:23:46.495 --> 00:23:47.870
By the way, that's
actually true.

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:48.940
It's not just non-decreasing.

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:49.982
It's actually increasing.

00:23:49.982 --> 00:23:50.830
Everybody got it?

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:53.680
OK, now, I'm going
to apply this--

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:54.880
I mean, this is ridiculous.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:56.890
But I'm going to apply
this fancy theorem.

00:23:56.890 --> 00:24:03.040
And it says, therefore,
if I have g1 of x up to gk

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:06.010
of x and if these
are all convex,

00:24:06.010 --> 00:24:09.430
then we conclude
using this thing here

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:11.650
that this thing is convex.

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.020
But that's the sum.

00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:17.050
So that's a pedantic
long-winded way

00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:19.330
to show that the sum
of convex functions

00:24:19.330 --> 00:24:21.670
is convex using the
composition rule.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:23.170
We can do it for max.

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:25.210
Let's do that just for
fun, just to show you

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:26.293
that it's the same thing.

00:24:26.293 --> 00:24:27.085
Here's my function.

00:24:31.030 --> 00:24:34.580
So let's talk about h.

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:37.830
Tell me about curvature of h.

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:40.210
Convex?

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:43.990
The max of a bunch
of-- yeah, it's convex.

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:46.330
Is it concave?

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:46.830
No.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:48.490
No, not in this case.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:52.470
Well, OK, if k is bigger
than 1, it's not concave.

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:53.160
It's not affine.

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:54.690
It's not concave.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:58.400
Tell me about monotonicity
of that function.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:01.230
It's non-decreasing
in each argument.

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:04.130
In other words, if I increase
any argument of the max,

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:06.960
the max, it can't go down.

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:08.270
So it's not increasing.

00:25:08.270 --> 00:25:11.270
It doesn't have to go up, unless
I grab one of the things that's

00:25:11.270 --> 00:25:12.600
actually active at the max.

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:13.280
And then I push it up.

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:14.155
Then the max goes up.

00:25:14.155 --> 00:25:14.960
Everybody got that?

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:15.920
So, OK.

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:18.540
So, again, this thing applies.

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:21.520
And so I didn't really
need to show you--

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:24.050
I guess if you wanted
a minimalist thing,

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:26.390
I didn't have to tell you
about the sum of convex

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:29.630
functions and the max
and things like that.

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:32.600
But I think humans think of
it more like these things,

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:33.860
and you sneak up to it.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:37.760
So, OK, everybody good?

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:40.040
So I mean, here
would be something

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:45.380
that's not totally
obvious is log sum

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:51.470
exp of a bunch of convex
functions is convex.

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:54.140
Or you might say
this, the softmax

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:56.960
of a bunch of convex
functions is convex.

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:59.400
These could be
losses when you're

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:00.660
fitting a statistical model.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:05.280
And this says that the log
sum exp is a convex function.

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:06.150
Why?

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:10.080
Because log sum exp
is A, convex; B,

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:12.090
increasing in all arguments.

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:14.100
And therefore, the
arguments could be

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:16.590
replaced with convex functions.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.570
Everybody got this?

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:20.670
So this is the composition rule.

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:24.040
It's extremely important.

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:27.052
And also-- I mean,
maybe I'm getting

00:26:27.052 --> 00:26:28.260
a little bit ahead of myself.

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:33.060
But this is actually-- you
can almost very easily imagine

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:37.290
something like this in some
object-oriented system,

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:42.300
where it would do very cool
things like to check f,

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:44.100
you just walk--

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:46.380
you walk the expression tree.

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:47.940
You look at f.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:51.720
Actually, all you have to
do is ask, tell me the--

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:54.990
tell me your curvature
and monotonicity.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.390
And it would say
something like, I'm convex

00:26:57.390 --> 00:26:59.720
and I am monotone decreasing.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:04.540
Well, you get something
for each argument.

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:06.670
Then you literally
iterate over the arguments

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:09.640
and call the same method
on each of the children

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:11.830
in the expression tree.

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:15.580
And this gives you
in a real language

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:20.560
one line that would allow you
to actually parse and verify

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:25.180
whether an entire expression
satisfies this, which means

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:27.610
that expression is convex.

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:30.790
The converse is
false, of course.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:34.960
It can fail-- the
composition rule can fail.

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:36.653
And yet, an expression
can be convex.

00:27:36.653 --> 00:27:38.320
So I'm actually getting
ahead of myself.

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:41.650
But this is already
starting to get--

00:27:41.650 --> 00:27:44.290
it's close to super
useful and interesting.

00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:46.200
OK, yeah.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:48.810
Why is max convex?

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:49.750
Oh.

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:50.250
Oh, yeah.

00:27:50.250 --> 00:27:52.350
So that's just--

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:55.260
I mean, you just have
to show it that the max

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.388
function is convex.

00:27:58.388 --> 00:27:59.430
I mean, this is circular.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:02.200
But I could say it's the max
of a bunch of linear functions,

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:04.508
which is x1 exp, but
that's weird because that's

00:28:04.508 --> 00:28:05.800
what we're trying to show here.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:08.280
So you'd actually just
have to show that.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:09.510
Yeah.

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:11.943
I mean, for things
that simple, you

00:28:11.943 --> 00:28:13.110
could actually do something.

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:14.370
You could take-- give me an x.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:15.150
Give me a y.

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:17.490
Give me a theta between 0 and 1.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.950
And then just write it down.

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:22.410
And actually, in that case,
in about two lines later,

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:23.730
you will have what you want.

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:25.290
So, OK.

00:28:25.290 --> 00:28:26.253
Yes.

00:28:26.253 --> 00:28:26.920
Just to clarify.

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:30.750
The summation of log g of ix,
is this supposed to be convex?

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:31.740
Is that a typo there?

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:32.520
Where?

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:37.140
The example is written
summation log g of i is concave.

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:38.430
No, that's correct.

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:40.307
Yeah, so let me explain why.

00:28:40.307 --> 00:28:40.890
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:41.932
That's a very good point.

00:28:41.932 --> 00:28:43.260
I didn't say this.

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:48.580
So by flipping the sign here,
you get a similar composition

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:50.500
theorem for concavity.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:53.200
Because something is
convex if minus f--

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:57.470
something is concave if
it's negative is convex.

00:28:57.470 --> 00:28:58.930
Then you apply this rule.

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:01.180
But when I negate a function,
all sorts of weird stuff

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:04.900
happens like its
monotonicity switches.

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:07.930
If it was increasing
before, now it's decreasing

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:10.110
and its curvature switches.

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:15.540
Yeah, so this one is using
an analogous composition

00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:17.460
rule for concave.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:19.470
So let me explain that.

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:21.670
A concave function of an--

00:29:21.670 --> 00:29:26.250
a concave increasing function of
a concave function is concave.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:31.360
A convex increasing function
of a convex function is convex.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.840
That's for one-- you
have one argument.

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:37.140
Then we could do the case
with multiple arguments.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:39.270
If you want, let's
actually just do one

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:42.090
for fun that's not obvious.

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:46.350
So here's a function.

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:48.390
This is x squared over y.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:51.120
And the domain is
going to be y positive.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:53.970
So this is the-- this is
that-- it's the simplest

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:55.920
function that most
people on the streets

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:58.410
would not know as convex.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:29:59.130
So that's convex.

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:00.870
So this is convex.

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.510
Let's talk about monotonicity.

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:06.240
Is it monotone in x?

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.880
That's his first argument.

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:10.040
Is it?

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:13.540
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.490
I don't think so.

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:19.600
Because x squared, it
actually has a negative slope

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:21.550
if x is negative.

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:25.210
Yeah, by the way, if x
is-- if x is non-negative,

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:27.445
then this is monotone
increasing in x.

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:35.432
OK, what is its
monotonicity and why?

00:30:35.432 --> 00:30:36.270
Decreasing.

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:37.450
It's what?

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:37.950
Decreasing.

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:39.720
It's decreasing.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:44.070
OK, so what this
means is if I want

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:46.350
to-- if I want to
satisfy those rules,

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:52.350
it says I can replace x
with an affine expression.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:54.000
That's the first thing I can do.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:58.870
And for y, I can replace it
with a concave expression.

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:00.630
So the following is a--

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:03.760
I could do something like this.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:06.350
Here you go.

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:10.520
Maybe I'll use z for this
and then x for my other one.

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:12.050
So this is y.

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:13.580
So I'll have this one.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:14.330
Here's a function.

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:15.290
Ready?

00:31:15.290 --> 00:31:19.910
1 transpose x squared, that's
the sum of the entries of x

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:22.910
then squared, divided by--

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:27.860
I don't know-- let's
say the minimum of 2

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:29.585
and square root of y.

00:31:34.288 --> 00:31:35.500
Yeah, in fact, you know what?

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:36.875
Let me do something
even weirder.

00:31:36.875 --> 00:31:42.730
Let me make this the
square root of x3.

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:43.630
Its third entry.

00:31:43.630 --> 00:31:45.560
There you go.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:48.130
There's some sick
function on our end.

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:50.920
I mean, you agree that it's
a very weird function, right?

00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:52.607
But it's completely
clear what it is.

00:31:52.607 --> 00:31:54.190
I have no idea where
it might come up.

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:56.620
This says, this is sum of
the entries of the vector

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:59.260
and square it and divide
it by the minimum of 2

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:01.480
and the square root of the
third entry of the vector.

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:02.530
Everybody got that?

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:05.063
Oh, and I'll have,
in this case--

00:32:05.063 --> 00:32:05.980
but it doesn't matter.

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:08.680
But I'll just say this.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:09.880
Everybody got the function?

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:14.170
Now, if I asked you, is
that convex or concave--

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:16.810
let me tell you something
things you would not want to do.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:18.310
You say, well,
here's x and here's y

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:20.890
and here's theta between 0
and 1, and then start arguing.

00:32:20.890 --> 00:32:22.900
You don't want to do that.

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:26.060
You don't want to take a
Hessian for many, many reasons.

00:32:26.060 --> 00:32:28.720
Number one, this is
not differentiable.

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:29.710
That's number one.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:32.330
Number two, even if it were, you
don't want to take a Hessian.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.050
Trust me.

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:36.540
Everybody cool?

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:40.110
And now, we're going to show
that this is convex just using

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:42.510
this rule over here.

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:44.238
So let's walk through that.

00:32:44.238 --> 00:32:45.030
How does that look?

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:46.572
So this is the kind
of thing you will

00:32:46.572 --> 00:32:49.680
be doing for-- you will be doing
this for eight weeks minimum.

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:50.962
So it goes like this.

00:32:50.962 --> 00:32:52.920
You're going to say,
well, I don't-- let's see.

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:54.370
You look at this function.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:55.650
It's convex.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:57.960
We know that.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:03.540
It is neither monotone nor
increasing or decreasing in z.

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:07.390
But I can put an
affine function there.

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:10.890
And that would be-- so
this is perfectly cool.

00:33:10.890 --> 00:33:14.100
This function is
decreasing in y.

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:18.300
So I can substitute for
y a concave function.

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:22.370
Now, is this concave?

00:33:22.370 --> 00:33:24.380
Let's, again, use
the composition.

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:26.510
Let's just go slow
with composition rules.

00:33:26.510 --> 00:33:30.760
Is the constant 2 concave?

00:33:30.760 --> 00:33:32.530
Yeah, it is.

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:35.330
How about square root of x3?

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:36.362
That's concave.

00:33:36.362 --> 00:33:38.570
And, in fact, if you want
to get real fancy about it,

00:33:38.570 --> 00:33:42.610
you'd say, oh, that's the
square root function precomposed

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:46.300
with a linear operator, a linear
function of x, which simply

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:47.560
pulls out to third entry.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:49.570
Everybody got-- I mean, that's
if you want to be real fancy.

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:50.920
Or you can just say,
nah, it's concave.

00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:51.420
It's fine.

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:53.230
Everybody got me?

00:33:53.230 --> 00:33:56.320
OK, how about is--
how about the minimum

00:33:56.320 --> 00:33:58.877
of two concave functions?

00:33:58.877 --> 00:34:00.460
Well, I should have
said this earlier.

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:02.830
The max of two convex
functions is convex.

00:34:02.830 --> 00:34:06.880
Turns out the min of two
concave functions is concave.

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:14.110
And you can verify that with
rules like this one, which

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:18.850
you probably know or something
like that, that the min of a, b

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:24.179
is something like minus the
max of minus a and minus b.

00:34:24.179 --> 00:34:25.179
I hope I got that right.

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:28.760
But it feels right.

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:32.230
If that's not right, something
really close to it is right.

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:34.179
And that's what I meant.

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:35.420
How about that?

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:36.960
OK, so I'm allowed.

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:38.870
You can't say that in
an undergrad class.

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:41.030
I can say that in a
grad class, for sure.

00:34:41.030 --> 00:34:43.370
I don't have any
problem with that.

00:34:43.370 --> 00:34:46.070
Everybody good on this?

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:48.500
OK, so basically then,
by the composition rule,

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:52.460
this crazy function
here is convex.

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:55.500
And basically, if you
didn't know about this--

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:58.160
I mean, all I would say
is best of luck to you

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:00.710
to show that that-- to recognize
that function as convex

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:02.040
or to show it.

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:03.200
Everybody got it?

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:07.430
So what we're doing here, we
refer to this as constructive

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:12.810
convex analysis, that we look
at a function or an expression.

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:16.550
And what we do is we try
to build it constructive.

00:35:16.550 --> 00:35:19.190
Literally, you could start at
the leaves of the expression

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:22.490
tree, like this
thing and that thing,

00:35:22.490 --> 00:35:25.250
and then actually literally
look at the expression tree,

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:28.490
walk up the tree,
and label every node

00:35:28.490 --> 00:35:33.620
as either convex,
concave, maybe affine.

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:35.480
And then just check
if this rule applies

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:37.022
at every node in
the expression tree.

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:45.520
OK, this is all this.

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:47.770
And I know you're seeing
this only for the first time.

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:49.517
But you will see
this so many times.

00:35:49.517 --> 00:35:50.350
It's not even funny.

00:35:50.350 --> 00:35:50.980
Yes.

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:52.360
Sorry, two question.

00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:58.870
Why is x squared over y not
monotonically increasing in x?

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:00.520
Because what if x is--

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:02.770
well, let's take--
here, I'll show you.

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:04.510
Let's take y equals 1.

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:08.200
And I'm going to take
z equals minus 1.

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:11.110
And this would evaluate
to the number 1.

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:13.180
And now, take z equals 0.

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:15.340
Well, now, it evaluates to 0.

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:15.940
Uh-oh.

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:19.400
Because z just went up and
the function value went down.

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:19.900
Oh.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:20.590
There you go.

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:23.380
Now, if I go from
0 to 1, it goes up.

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:26.440
By the way-- very good point--

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:29.650
since we're doing
sine arithmetic

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:34.070
on the 0-th, first,
and second derivative.

00:36:34.070 --> 00:36:40.220
Suppose I told you ahead of
time that z was non-negative.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:43.130
If you knew z was
non-negative, then

00:36:43.130 --> 00:36:45.680
you could actually
classify this as being

00:36:45.680 --> 00:36:49.040
increasing in the argument z.

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:50.300
Everybody got that?

00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:54.520
So that's sign-dependent
monotonicity.

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:56.180
Everybody get?

00:36:56.180 --> 00:37:00.290
OK, so that is the idea.

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:02.680
OK.

00:37:02.680 --> 00:37:06.790
OK, so there'll be a
lot of stuff on that.

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:10.450
This is actually-- this is
the basis of actually pretty

00:37:10.450 --> 00:37:12.272
much all software for
convex optimization

00:37:12.272 --> 00:37:14.230
is based on something
called disciplined convex

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:16.810
programming, which
means absolutely nothing

00:37:16.810 --> 00:37:19.720
more than everything
you write down

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:23.200
can be verified using
that one composition rule.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:25.192
That's it.

00:37:25.192 --> 00:37:26.650
So in some sense,
that's the only--

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:28.010
there's only one rule
you need to know,

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:29.290
which is the composition rule.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:32.290
And now, you're good to go.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:34.360
So, OK.

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:37.940
All right, we'll look at
a bunch of other things.

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:40.550
And there are some subtleties.

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:43.150
So first, remember
that if I have

00:37:43.150 --> 00:37:48.790
a function of two variables
and I take the maximum over one

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:50.278
of the variables--

00:37:50.278 --> 00:37:51.070
we did that before.

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:52.840
It was the supremum over y.

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:55.150
And the fact is y didn't
even have to be a vector.

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:57.250
It could be anything at all.

00:37:57.250 --> 00:38:01.480
But if you take the max-- so
the maximum of f of xy where

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:06.050
f is convex and x for each y is
convex, what's cool is the same

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:07.040
is true for a minimum.

00:38:07.040 --> 00:38:10.433
But the conditions are
different and not totally

00:38:10.433 --> 00:38:11.100
straightforward.

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:13.310
So here's what they are.

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:19.370
It says if f of x and y is
jointly convex in x and y

00:38:19.370 --> 00:38:24.720
and then I minimize over
y over a convex set--

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:28.470
that's this thing-- then
this thing is convex.

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:31.050
And some people call
this-- oh, one name--

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:34.500
oh, a great name for this
is partial minimization.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:37.020
Because what it
means is you have

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:39.930
a function of two
variables, their vectors.

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.640
And I minimize over 1.

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:44.252
So that's called-- yeah, I
don't minimize over everything.

00:38:44.252 --> 00:38:46.710
It's called partial-- so you
would say something like this,

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:51.565
partial minimization
preserves convexity.

00:38:51.565 --> 00:38:54.190
So by the way, this would be the
principle behind, for example,

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:56.700
dynamic programming.

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:59.220
So that's what dynamic
programming does.

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:02.670
It's to minimize a big
old function like this.

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:05.310
You start by-- you
minimize over one variable.

00:39:05.310 --> 00:39:08.220
And then what you end up with is
the value function or something

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:08.820
like that.

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:11.580
And this would tell you things
about that being convex or not.

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:14.020
OK, yeah.

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:16.980
Just to make sure, when
you say jointly convex,

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.040
it doesn't mean that it has
to be convex in x separately

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:21.390
and then y.

00:39:21.390 --> 00:39:23.550
It can be concave
in one of them.

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:25.230
No, no.

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:28.390
So jointly convex
is very strong.

00:39:28.390 --> 00:39:32.370
So if I have a function
of two variables, x and y,

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:34.950
it could be convex
in x for each y.

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:38.220
And it could be convex
in y for each x.

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:42.450
But it's not convex
jointly in xy.

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:44.760
Someone want to
give me an example,

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:47.385
simple example of that?

00:39:47.385 --> 00:39:48.350
xy.

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:48.890
xy.

00:39:48.890 --> 00:39:49.440
There you go.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:49.940
Perfect.

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:53.210
So the function xy,
they're both scalars,

00:39:53.210 --> 00:39:54.650
it's literally the product.

00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:57.590
If I fix y, it's linear in x.

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:59.630
If I fix x, it's linear in y.

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:01.130
It's called a bilinear function.

00:40:01.130 --> 00:40:04.070
Or actually, it's
just a quadratic form.

00:40:04.070 --> 00:40:06.920
But it's definitely
convex in x for each y.

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:08.240
It's convex in y for each x.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:13.580
And it is definitely not
convex jointly in xy.

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:14.750
So it means weird things.

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:18.140
It says that if you look at
the function in the xy plane,

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:20.120
it says as long as
you're either only

00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:23.360
going vertical or horizontal,
it's got the right curvature.

00:40:23.360 --> 00:40:24.800
You go this way; curves up.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.740
You go this way; curves up,
which I guess right this way.

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:32.450
But you go off that grid, if
you go at a 45-degree angle

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:34.940
or something and it does not--

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:38.090
well, the function xy
certainly does not curve up.

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:40.550
It actually curves down.

00:40:40.550 --> 00:40:43.080
So that makes sense?

00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:44.930
So there's a strong--

00:40:44.930 --> 00:40:48.860
so to say jointly convex
is a strong condition.

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:51.380
OK, so here are some examples.

00:40:51.380 --> 00:40:54.260
Oh, here's a famous one.

00:40:54.260 --> 00:40:58.290
Two vectors, x and y--
and I write down a--

00:40:58.290 --> 00:40:59.630
this is a quadratic form.

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:02.930
In fact, what that is
is it's a quadratic form

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:05.600
in x stacked on top of y.

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:11.150
So that function is nothing
more than xy transpose.

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:13.580
And then I'll have to
fill in the matrices

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:16.125
when I see what I had over here.

00:41:16.125 --> 00:41:17.000
Let's see what I did.

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:22.490
Oh, A, B, B,
transpose C. So that's

00:41:22.490 --> 00:41:28.460
a quadratic form in
the stacked vector xy.

00:41:28.460 --> 00:41:32.270
And what we're going to
do is we're going to say--

00:41:32.270 --> 00:41:34.220
oh, when is this--
when is this--

00:41:34.220 --> 00:41:36.095
when is this function
convex jointly?

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:40.940
Matrix.

00:41:40.940 --> 00:41:41.440
What's that?

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:44.500
Yeah, this block
2 by 2 matrix has

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:46.660
to be positive semi-definite.

00:41:46.660 --> 00:41:48.890
That's the condition on
which this is convex.

00:41:48.890 --> 00:41:53.650
Now, let's minimize over y here.

00:41:53.650 --> 00:41:56.470
Well, how do you-- how do you
minimize this thing over y?

00:41:56.470 --> 00:41:59.500
I mean, we just take a gradient.

00:41:59.500 --> 00:42:00.650
You take a gradient.

00:42:00.650 --> 00:42:04.420
And here, you get a term,
which is like 2B transpose x.

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:08.080
And over there, you get 2Cy.

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:09.490
You solve that.

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:11.140
And you work out what it is.

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:13.030
And you end up--
when you find out

00:42:13.030 --> 00:42:16.000
what y is, you plug it
back into this thing.

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:17.290
And you get this thing.

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:18.920
So you get x transpose.

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:20.020
So this is--

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:21.880
I take this function
of two variables.

00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:26.620
If I minimize over y for
each x, I end up number one,

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:27.870
with a quadratic form.

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:29.620
So you could even say
something like this.

00:42:29.620 --> 00:42:33.910
Quadratic forms are closed
under partial minimization.

00:42:33.910 --> 00:42:38.680
That's a perfectly
reasonable statement to make.

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:40.400
But not only it's kind of cool.

00:42:40.400 --> 00:42:43.580
If the original
quadratic form was PSD.

00:42:43.580 --> 00:42:45.123
So is this one.

00:42:45.123 --> 00:42:46.040
And that's got a name.

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:48.170
That's called the
Schur complement.

00:42:48.170 --> 00:42:51.807
And you've doubtless all seen--

00:42:51.807 --> 00:42:53.390
it'd be very difficult
to take classes

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:54.890
where you don't see this.

00:42:54.890 --> 00:42:59.390
This is conditioning on
a Gaussian random vector.

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:01.850
In electrical engineering,
this is like taking a circuit

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:05.030
and imposing conditions on
some of the ports or nodes.

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:06.680
In mechanical
engineering-- it comes up

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:08.690
in every field there
is-- economics.

00:43:08.690 --> 00:43:10.700
I can't think of
a legitimate field

00:43:10.700 --> 00:43:13.610
where the Schur complement
doesn't come up.

00:43:17.090 --> 00:43:19.370
And sometimes
people call it out.

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:20.660
Sometimes they don't.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:21.260
I don't know.

00:43:21.260 --> 00:43:22.760
Actually, how many
people have heard

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:25.340
it called out as a
Schur complement before?

00:43:25.340 --> 00:43:27.290
OK, handful.

00:43:27.290 --> 00:43:29.690
You'll have seen it somewhere
or seen something like this

00:43:29.690 --> 00:43:33.380
probably with some
hideous notation in.

00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:36.258
What field was it called out in?

00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:41.080
In the back.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:43.270
In my case, numerical
linear algebra.

00:43:43.270 --> 00:43:44.890
OK, in numerical linear algebra.

00:43:44.890 --> 00:43:45.920
OK, good.

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:46.660
OK, good.

00:43:46.660 --> 00:43:48.725
All right.

00:43:48.725 --> 00:43:51.100
And you may know that it's a
basic fact of linear algebra

00:43:51.100 --> 00:43:53.350
that if a matrix is
positive semi-definite,

00:43:53.350 --> 00:43:57.190
so is a Schur complement or
positive definite or negative.

00:43:57.190 --> 00:44:00.460
OK, everybody got this?

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:04.680
OK, that's the
minimization rule.

00:44:04.680 --> 00:44:08.050
Now, we get to the
perspective rule.

00:44:08.050 --> 00:44:09.330
And so this is related.

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:12.060
If you remember the
perspective mapping,

00:44:12.060 --> 00:44:14.250
takes a vector with
the last entry positive

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:15.630
and divides everything by it.

00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:18.840
And then we looked at
linear fractional mappings.

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:21.030
And I mumbled about
how that's basically

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:23.820
the actual generic mapping
you have in vision.

00:44:23.820 --> 00:44:27.090
Like, if you have a camera
system that looks at 3D

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:29.760
and you look at the actual image
you get on the sensor, which

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:34.080
is 2D, it's actually got this
perspective mapping in it.

00:44:34.080 --> 00:44:39.900
OK, so the perspective
of a function is this.

00:44:42.870 --> 00:44:44.490
You take a function f.

00:44:44.490 --> 00:44:48.338
And it's a function
now of a new variable,

00:44:48.338 --> 00:44:50.130
which is going to be
positive and a scalar.

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:54.900
So g of x and t
is tf of x over t.

00:44:54.900 --> 00:44:57.660
So that's called
the perspective.

00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:58.920
Here's the cool part.

00:44:58.920 --> 00:45:03.550
If a function is convex,
so is its perspective.

00:45:03.550 --> 00:45:06.400
And, again, this is not--
that one is not totally

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:07.600
straightforward to show.

00:45:07.600 --> 00:45:09.520
But it's true.

00:45:09.520 --> 00:45:11.800
And this is not-- this one is--

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:13.720
this is just not obvious.

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:16.000
So let's look at some examples.

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:17.560
Oh, here's one.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:21.430
x transpose x is the sum of
the squares of a vector--

00:45:21.430 --> 00:45:23.230
of the entries of a vector.

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:25.940
It's convex.

00:45:25.940 --> 00:45:29.050
Well, let's figure out what is
the-- what you do is you just

00:45:29.050 --> 00:45:29.890
plug in--

00:45:29.890 --> 00:45:32.830
wherever you see x,
plug- n x over t here.

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:36.070
So you have x over t
transpose x over t.

00:45:36.070 --> 00:45:38.800
So that becomes x transpose
x divided by t squared.

00:45:38.800 --> 00:45:40.600
And then you multiply
by t in the front.

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:41.320
And you get this.

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:42.400
And you go, hey, that's cool.

00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:43.317
You know what that is?

00:45:43.317 --> 00:45:46.990
That's sum xi squared over t.

00:45:46.990 --> 00:45:51.730
So it's a sum of these quadratic
over linear functions, which

00:45:51.730 --> 00:45:54.520
you know to be convex.

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:57.400
But you found that out without
any of the pain or something

00:45:57.400 --> 00:45:58.048
like that.

00:45:58.048 --> 00:45:59.590
I mean, actually,
we showed that by--

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:02.840
I think there, we
resorted to Hessians.

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:05.150
So, OK.

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:05.930
Here's one.

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:07.400
Let's take the
negative logarithm.

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:11.510
That's convex because
the log is concave.

00:46:11.510 --> 00:46:13.340
So we take negative logarithm.

00:46:13.340 --> 00:46:15.740
And then let's form the--

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:20.480
if we form the
perspective of this,

00:46:20.480 --> 00:46:26.840
It's minus t log x over t,
which you might recognize.

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:28.340
That's the relative entropy.

00:46:28.340 --> 00:46:30.050
And then from that,
it would be related

00:46:30.050 --> 00:46:32.467
to things like KL divergence
and all sorts of other stuff.

00:46:32.467 --> 00:46:34.133
I mean, it doesn't
matter if you haven't

00:46:34.133 --> 00:46:35.450
seen these functions before.

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:37.400
But that just gives
you a real quick way

00:46:37.400 --> 00:46:39.650
to show that some of
these-- that these functions

00:46:39.650 --> 00:46:40.580
are convex.

00:46:40.580 --> 00:46:42.470
Everybody got this?

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:45.310
So, OK.

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:46.810
And let's see.

00:46:46.810 --> 00:46:48.760
So I think I'll--

00:46:48.760 --> 00:46:51.670
anyway, so it's a very--
and by the way, sometimes

00:46:51.670 --> 00:46:54.040
this actually has
very interesting

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:57.640
practical interpretations
of what gx over t is.

00:46:57.640 --> 00:46:59.470
And we'll assign some
homework problems

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:01.360
that show you where
it would come up

00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:03.310
in various practical things.

00:47:03.310 --> 00:47:07.780
I mean, aside from vision
or something like that.

00:47:07.780 --> 00:47:10.660
OK, next up is conjugate.

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:12.730
And I understand
that all of these

00:47:12.730 --> 00:47:14.200
are completely without context.

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:19.870
So if you're asking like who
cares about this, completely

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:21.040
reasonable question to ask.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:22.270
Totally reasonable.

00:47:22.270 --> 00:47:23.230
But anyway.

00:47:23.230 --> 00:47:24.940
And my answer to
that is you will

00:47:24.940 --> 00:47:27.400
in the next couple of weeks.

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:30.010
And also, once you start--
once you see these things,

00:47:30.010 --> 00:47:31.570
you will start seeing
them everywhere

00:47:31.570 --> 00:47:34.130
you go like in your other
classes and things like that.

00:47:34.130 --> 00:47:38.770
So I mean, this is completely
central-- it's absolutely

00:47:38.770 --> 00:47:39.820
central to economics.

00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:42.280
It's completely
central to statistics.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:44.120
It just comes up all the time.

00:47:44.120 --> 00:47:45.680
It's called the
conjugate function.

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:48.600
And it looks like this.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:50.600
So it's this.

00:47:50.600 --> 00:47:51.740
I have a function f.

00:47:51.740 --> 00:47:54.680
Does not have to be convex.

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:59.210
And f star y or f asterisk y--

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:01.550
that's the pretty much
universal notation

00:48:01.550 --> 00:48:03.140
for the conjugate function.

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:04.010
It's actually this.

00:48:04.010 --> 00:48:05.480
It's really interesting.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:09.830
You should maximize--
y is the argument here.

00:48:09.830 --> 00:48:13.790
You should maximize this y
transpose x minus f of x.

00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:16.520
So that's what this is.

00:48:16.520 --> 00:48:19.760
And actually, this is crazy.

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:25.470
Let's show that this is
convex even when f is not.

00:48:25.470 --> 00:48:31.260
So the conjugate of a non-convex
function is going to be convex.

00:48:31.260 --> 00:48:34.530
But let's figure
out-- let's see why.

00:48:34.530 --> 00:48:35.790
Any suggestions?

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:40.370
Is it Hessian time?

00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:41.030
No.

00:48:41.030 --> 00:48:42.200
I mean, duh.

00:48:42.200 --> 00:48:44.760
OK, so how do you do it?

00:48:44.760 --> 00:48:49.520
How do I show that that thing
is a convex function of y?

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:51.270
Yeah.

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:53.240
The supremum of
affine functions.

00:48:53.240 --> 00:48:53.820
There you go.

00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:55.440
So here's what you do.

00:48:55.440 --> 00:48:56.630
You look at this thing.

00:48:56.630 --> 00:48:59.330
And it looks really complicated
like f is some incredibly

00:48:59.330 --> 00:49:00.940
complicated nonlinear thing.

00:49:00.940 --> 00:49:03.180
I don't even know what it is.

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:05.810
Then what this says
is the following--

00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:11.150
for each fixed x, for
each x in the domain of f,

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:15.120
what kind of function is y
transpose x minus f of x?

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:17.070
It's affine.

00:49:17.070 --> 00:49:20.430
This is a supremum
of affine functions.

00:49:20.430 --> 00:49:22.020
And it could only be convex.

00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:27.060
And so I could have
really messed with you.

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:28.557
If somebody walked
up on the street

00:49:28.557 --> 00:49:31.140
and they showed f and they wrote
it out in some horrible thing

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:34.170
and it had hyperbolic cosines
and sines and all that,

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:35.460
they'd say, is this convex?

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:37.930
You'd go, like, oh,
my god, I don't know.

00:49:37.930 --> 00:49:40.750
But the point, that you
just have to remain calm,

00:49:40.750 --> 00:49:44.535
look at it, and realize
that it's like a conjugate.

00:49:44.535 --> 00:49:46.800
So that's a conjugate.

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:49.020
And this is going
to come up again.

00:49:49.020 --> 00:49:51.570
It's going to be central
when we cover duality.

00:49:51.570 --> 00:49:52.840
But it also comes up a lot.

00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:54.298
And I just give
you a quick example

00:49:54.298 --> 00:49:55.800
of where this would come up.

00:49:55.800 --> 00:49:59.710
Let's suppose you run a
business or something like that.

00:49:59.710 --> 00:50:07.320
And x is an amount of various
products that you will produce.

00:50:07.320 --> 00:50:10.140
So it's a vector of, you
have n different products.

00:50:10.140 --> 00:50:13.330
And you're going to produce
x1, a product 1, x2, and so on.

00:50:13.330 --> 00:50:14.310
Everybody got it?

00:50:14.310 --> 00:50:17.440
That is going to
cost you f of x.

00:50:17.440 --> 00:50:21.280
So that is your production
cost for producing

00:50:21.280 --> 00:50:24.850
so many tons of this, so many
units of that, and so on.

00:50:24.850 --> 00:50:26.680
Everybody got it?

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:31.590
y, we interpret as
the market prices.

00:50:31.590 --> 00:50:32.640
So these are the--

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:36.780
y1 through yn are the prices
for products 1 through n.

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:40.350
y transpose x is
your gross revenue

00:50:40.350 --> 00:50:41.670
for the products you've made.

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:43.590
Everybody got this?

00:50:43.590 --> 00:50:47.010
OK, so this is your profit.

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:53.218
It's your gross revenue minus
the cost to actually produce

00:50:53.218 --> 00:50:53.760
those things.

00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:55.260
Everybody following this?

00:50:55.260 --> 00:50:59.620
So the conjugate
then is very simple.

00:50:59.620 --> 00:51:05.400
It is the optimal profit as
a function of the prices.

00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:06.850
Everybody got this?

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:09.058
We're going to come back to
this in much more detail.

00:51:09.058 --> 00:51:11.392
But maybe just planting some
of these seeds in your head

00:51:11.392 --> 00:51:12.930
is not at all a bad
thing right now.

00:51:15.540 --> 00:51:17.250
So that's the conjugate.

00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:20.880
And what's that?

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:22.320
OK, good.

00:51:22.320 --> 00:51:23.257
Everybody got it?

00:51:23.257 --> 00:51:25.590
So I mean, other than that,
it has no meaning right now.

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:27.048
It's going to have
a meaning later.

00:51:27.048 --> 00:51:28.770
Don't worry.

00:51:28.770 --> 00:51:29.270
Yeah.

00:51:29.270 --> 00:51:30.960
Could you just
explain the graph?

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:32.020
What's that?

00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:34.530
Could you explain the graph?

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:35.520
Yeah, I could.

00:51:35.520 --> 00:51:36.390
Yeah, yeah.

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:39.460
So what you do, if you want
to do this graphically,

00:51:39.460 --> 00:51:42.370
here's f here.

00:51:42.370 --> 00:51:46.170
And what you do is you form xy.

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:49.890
That's these different--
that's this line here.

00:51:49.890 --> 00:51:52.200
That's this line.

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:55.050
And then what you're doing is
you move this line up and down

00:51:55.050 --> 00:51:57.300
until it just touches the graph.

00:51:57.300 --> 00:51:59.910
And then it turns out
that point right there is

00:51:59.910 --> 00:52:02.880
the negative of the conjugate.

00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:05.470
So that's the graphical
interpretation.

00:52:05.470 --> 00:52:08.170
I mean, if that helps you.

00:52:08.170 --> 00:52:11.560
I think I've decided it doesn't
really help me, the graph.

00:52:11.560 --> 00:52:14.193
So it's fine.

00:52:14.193 --> 00:52:15.360
OK, so that's the conjugate.

00:52:15.360 --> 00:52:19.050
By the way, this comes up
in lots of other fields too,

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:20.620
I mean, statistics, probability.

00:52:20.620 --> 00:52:22.270
I go on and on and on.

00:52:22.270 --> 00:52:30.960
Let me ask-- when you use
the language conjugate,

00:52:30.960 --> 00:52:34.230
it conjures up certain ideas.

00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:36.780
Because conjugate is used
in math in lots of cases.

00:52:36.780 --> 00:52:40.260
And mostly-- I mean, here's
one, complex conjugate

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:43.260
of a complex number.

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:47.340
So Hermitian conjugate
of a complex matrix.

00:52:47.340 --> 00:52:50.910
These are types of conjugates.

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:53.100
What is an attribute you
think of when someone

00:52:53.100 --> 00:52:57.040
uses the conjugate word?

00:52:57.040 --> 00:52:58.130
What's that?

00:52:58.130 --> 00:52:58.950
Reversible.

00:52:58.950 --> 00:52:59.640
I missed that.

00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:00.660
It's reversible.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:02.220
It's reversible.

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:04.680
Yeah, yeah, OK.

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:06.360
Let's be a little
bit more specific.

00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:07.650
How do you reverse it?

00:53:07.650 --> 00:53:10.230
If you find a
concave, once you get,

00:53:10.230 --> 00:53:11.770
it will return the original.

00:53:11.770 --> 00:53:13.170
I like that.

00:53:13.170 --> 00:53:17.280
So typically, when you
use the word conjugate,

00:53:17.280 --> 00:53:19.860
there's an implication that
the conjugate of the conjugate

00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:21.420
is the original thing.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:24.230
Everybody got that?

00:53:24.230 --> 00:53:27.980
So the conjugate of the
conjugate of a complex number

00:53:27.980 --> 00:53:29.872
is the same number back again.

00:53:29.872 --> 00:53:32.330
Because you switch the sign on
the imaginary part, and then

00:53:32.330 --> 00:53:33.200
you do it again.

00:53:33.200 --> 00:53:37.390
OK, do you think it's true that
the conjugate of a conjugate

00:53:37.390 --> 00:53:43.350
of a function is the function?

00:53:43.350 --> 00:53:43.850
No.

00:53:43.850 --> 00:53:44.530
Why not?

00:53:44.530 --> 00:53:48.660
Because f star is always
convex yet is not necessarily.

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:49.170
OK, yeah.

00:53:49.170 --> 00:53:52.150
But take it one more
step. f star is convex.

00:53:52.150 --> 00:53:55.630
So f star star is convex.

00:53:55.630 --> 00:53:59.760
So if f was not convex to start
with like this function here,

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:02.340
then there's no way
f star star equals f.

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:03.660
You're right.

00:54:03.660 --> 00:54:06.100
So actually, f star star
is a really cool thing.

00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:10.210
It's called the convex
envelope of a function.

00:54:10.210 --> 00:54:13.110
So it's the largest convex
function that fits under it.

00:54:13.110 --> 00:54:14.510
So I'll just draw that.

00:54:14.510 --> 00:54:16.260
This is advanced, and
I probably shouldn't

00:54:16.260 --> 00:54:17.302
be going there right now.

00:54:17.302 --> 00:54:19.920
But too late now.

00:54:19.920 --> 00:54:23.080
So here's your function.

00:54:23.080 --> 00:54:25.380
And I'll show you
what f star star is.

00:54:25.380 --> 00:54:27.993
It's called the convex envelope
or lots of other things.

00:54:27.993 --> 00:54:29.160
It's actually this function.

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:30.220
It's really cool.

00:54:30.220 --> 00:54:32.880
It is the largest
convex function

00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:35.430
that fits under this thing.

00:54:35.430 --> 00:54:36.570
And it's going to be this.

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:38.250
It's going to look
like that, then

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:40.200
it's going to go
like that, and then

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:42.780
it's going to go up like that.

00:54:42.780 --> 00:54:45.840
Or here's a really
cool way to say it.

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:48.300
If you take the epigraph
of the function--

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:50.010
that's everything up here--

00:54:50.010 --> 00:54:54.810
then take the convex hull of the
epigraph-- that's this thing--

00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:58.000
that's the epigraph of f star
star, which is the conjugate.

00:54:58.000 --> 00:55:01.467
So sorry, this is too advanced.

00:55:01.467 --> 00:55:02.550
I didn't mean to go there.

00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:03.550
But it is kind of cool.

00:55:03.550 --> 00:55:04.800
So I don't mind mentioning it.

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:05.865
Yes.

00:55:05.865 --> 00:55:10.050
Its convex is f star
star the same as f?

00:55:10.050 --> 00:55:11.320
Yes, awesome.

00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:11.820
Yes.

00:55:11.820 --> 00:55:15.270
So if f is convex--

00:55:15.270 --> 00:55:17.460
and there's a small
technical condition--

00:55:17.460 --> 00:55:19.710
then f star star is f.

00:55:19.710 --> 00:55:21.810
And that's going to be
the basis of a whole lot

00:55:21.810 --> 00:55:25.860
of different things
and interesting stuff.

00:55:25.860 --> 00:55:28.992
OK, so let's look
at some examples.

00:55:28.992 --> 00:55:30.450
How do you work
out the conjugates?

00:55:30.450 --> 00:55:32.790
A lot of times, you just
have to get-- you just

00:55:32.790 --> 00:55:37.292
have to get your hands dirty and
figure out how to solve this.

00:55:37.292 --> 00:55:39.000
It's defined by this
optimization problem

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:40.110
where you maximize.

00:55:40.110 --> 00:55:42.030
And so, for example,
let's find--

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:43.380
here's the negative logarithm.

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:45.660
Let's find the conjugate of it.

00:55:45.660 --> 00:55:47.580
Well, you have to
maximize over y, which

00:55:47.580 --> 00:55:50.530
is a scalar, xy plus log x.

00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:53.110
And, honestly, the way
you do this, you just

00:55:53.110 --> 00:55:54.280
draw some pictures.

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:55.550
And you will find out.

00:55:55.550 --> 00:55:58.420
For example, if
y is 0 or bigger,

00:55:58.420 --> 00:56:01.090
this supremum is infinity.

00:56:01.090 --> 00:56:03.970
Because well, look, if y is 3--

00:56:03.970 --> 00:56:05.650
this is ridiculous--
what happens

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:08.740
is this thing is
monotone increasing in y.

00:56:08.740 --> 00:56:12.220
And you've been
asked to maximize it.

00:56:12.220 --> 00:56:14.110
So you get plus infinity.

00:56:14.110 --> 00:56:15.880
If y is negative, it's not.

00:56:15.880 --> 00:56:18.010
And you just draw a picture
and take a derivative.

00:56:18.010 --> 00:56:19.430
And you can work out what it is.

00:56:19.430 --> 00:56:20.300
I'm not going to do that.

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:22.383
But that's what-- but you
get something like this.

00:56:22.383 --> 00:56:25.360
So roughly speaking,
the conjugate of a log

00:56:25.360 --> 00:56:26.290
looks like a log.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:27.880
I mean, the sign got flipped.

00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:29.440
And there's a minus 1 offset.

00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:31.570
But that's that.

00:56:31.570 --> 00:56:36.280
If you have a quadratic
form, 1/2 x transpose Qx

00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:38.560
with a positive
definite Q, then it

00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:40.720
turns out that the
conjugate is really cool.

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:43.690
It's given by the-- it's
also a quadratic form given

00:56:43.690 --> 00:56:46.390
by the inverse matrix.

00:56:46.390 --> 00:56:49.360
So everybody got this?

00:56:49.360 --> 00:56:52.960
So these are just basic things.

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:59.300
OK, we're now going
to cover two more

00:56:59.300 --> 00:57:01.080
generalizations of convexity.

00:57:01.080 --> 00:57:04.230
So first, let me give
you some background,

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:08.640
that there's a whole
sub industry in math

00:57:08.640 --> 00:57:11.040
of generalizing convexity.

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:14.460
And you have pseudoconvexity
and quasiconvexity.

00:57:14.460 --> 00:57:15.330
And I don't know.

00:57:15.330 --> 00:57:18.780
There's all these different
types of generalizations.

00:57:18.780 --> 00:57:21.510
And it's totally fine.

00:57:21.510 --> 00:57:23.960
It's not hurting anybody.

00:57:23.960 --> 00:57:26.100
And then usually, on
page 300 of the book,

00:57:26.100 --> 00:57:28.110
you'll get this
horrible diagram that

00:57:28.110 --> 00:57:30.750
says, if something is
quasiconvex and triple

00:57:30.750 --> 00:57:33.990
pseudoconvex and blah, blah,
blah, then it's also this.

00:57:33.990 --> 00:57:37.110
And it's a big diagram
that shows you all this.

00:57:37.110 --> 00:57:41.520
OK, so what we're
going to do here

00:57:41.520 --> 00:57:45.030
is we've selected the
two generalizations that

00:57:45.030 --> 00:57:47.520
are actually useful.

00:57:47.520 --> 00:57:49.680
And they are quasiconvexity.

00:57:49.680 --> 00:57:50.625
That's this one.

00:57:50.625 --> 00:57:52.500
And we're going to talk
about one more, which

00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:55.920
is log convexity,
which is almost not

00:57:55.920 --> 00:57:59.220
quite a generalization.

00:57:59.220 --> 00:58:00.600
But it is kind of.

00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:02.790
But this one is.

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:04.200
So that's what
we're going to do.

00:58:04.200 --> 00:58:06.325
So I'm just saying, if you
go to Wikipedia and type

00:58:06.325 --> 00:58:09.960
in quasiconvexity, you'll
find out there's pseudoconvex.

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:12.935
And then someone showed that
if it's pseudoconvex and blah,

00:58:12.935 --> 00:58:15.060
blah, blah, blah, blah,
then it has to be-- anyway,

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:16.030
it's very complicated.

00:58:16.030 --> 00:58:17.610
So, OK.

00:58:17.610 --> 00:58:19.140
Here it is.

00:58:19.140 --> 00:58:22.890
A function is quasiconvex if its
sublevel sets are all convex.

00:58:22.890 --> 00:58:23.880
Period.

00:58:23.880 --> 00:58:26.340
Oh, another name for
this is unimodal.

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:29.250
So that's a very common name.

00:58:29.250 --> 00:58:31.980
And here's an example
of a function in R.

00:58:31.980 --> 00:58:33.360
It's definitely not convex.

00:58:33.360 --> 00:58:36.330
I mean, look, there's negative
curvature right there.

00:58:36.330 --> 00:58:37.740
So that's not convex.

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:38.820
It is quasiconvex.

00:58:38.820 --> 00:58:41.460
And the way you check if
it's quasi convex is you

00:58:41.460 --> 00:58:43.740
just take your level set.

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:46.050
What is the sublevel
set for this level?

00:58:49.280 --> 00:58:51.800
What is the set of x for
which the function is

00:58:51.800 --> 00:58:54.078
smaller than this value?

00:58:54.078 --> 00:58:56.170
[INAUDIBLE]

00:58:56.170 --> 00:58:58.300
No, I'm down here.

00:58:58.300 --> 00:58:59.450
It's empty.

00:58:59.450 --> 00:59:00.520
See, it's the empty set.

00:59:00.520 --> 00:59:01.990
Is the empty set convex?

00:59:01.990 --> 00:59:04.120
Yeah, so that's cool.

00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:07.053
If I go-- if I
put something that

00:59:07.053 --> 00:59:08.470
just touches the
bottom of this, I

00:59:08.470 --> 00:59:10.330
get a Singleton, the minimizer.

00:59:10.330 --> 00:59:11.390
That's convex.

00:59:11.390 --> 00:59:15.430
Now, as I raise this
level, you get an interval.

00:59:15.430 --> 00:59:16.810
But intervals are convex.

00:59:16.810 --> 00:59:20.320
What happens is if I raise
it at the value beta,

00:59:20.320 --> 00:59:23.470
I get a half-infinite interval.

00:59:23.470 --> 00:59:26.900
That's also convex.

00:59:26.900 --> 00:59:29.630
So unimodal is
another name for it.

00:59:29.630 --> 00:59:32.050
And basically, for a
function of one variable,

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:37.330
something is quasiconvex
if, roughly speaking,

00:59:37.330 --> 00:59:38.650
it's decreasing.

00:59:38.650 --> 00:59:41.770
There's a point where it's
decreasing up to that point

00:59:41.770 --> 00:59:44.500
and then increasing above it.

00:59:44.500 --> 00:59:47.980
It's also more interesting
in the vector case.

00:59:47.980 --> 00:59:48.911
Yeah.

00:59:48.911 --> 00:59:51.070
What is a
half-infinite interval?

00:59:51.070 --> 00:59:52.780
What's a half-infinite interval?

00:59:52.780 --> 00:59:54.580
It is this.

00:59:54.580 --> 00:59:58.030
It would look like
something like this.

00:59:58.030 --> 00:59:58.660
There.

00:59:58.660 --> 01:00:00.190
That's a half-infinite interval.

01:00:00.190 --> 01:00:02.950
It's everything less
than or equal to 3.

01:00:02.950 --> 01:00:06.170
That's an example of a
half-infinite interval.

01:00:06.170 --> 01:00:08.810
Actually, do people
even say that?

01:00:08.810 --> 01:00:09.680
Infinite interval?

01:00:09.680 --> 01:00:10.370
I don't know.

01:00:10.370 --> 01:00:12.080
I made that up, that name.

01:00:12.080 --> 01:00:14.960
So it might not be a technical--

01:00:14.960 --> 01:00:16.880
but OK.

01:00:16.880 --> 01:00:19.430
Everybody got this?

01:00:19.430 --> 01:00:21.362
So that's what a
quasiconvex function is.

01:00:21.362 --> 01:00:22.820
And by the way,
it's actually super

01:00:22.820 --> 01:00:27.170
interesting suppose f is
a function that we want

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:30.110
to minimize like it's
an objective what's

01:00:30.110 --> 01:00:34.160
really cool about this says then
a sublevel set you can describe

01:00:34.160 --> 01:00:40.190
as all the choices that are as
good as or better than, let's

01:00:40.190 --> 01:00:41.120
say, alpha.

01:00:41.120 --> 01:00:42.770
Everybody got that?

01:00:42.770 --> 01:00:44.960
f is whatever your objective
is in your problem.

01:00:44.960 --> 01:00:46.610
Let's say it's a loss function.

01:00:46.610 --> 01:00:48.290
And you're fitting something.

01:00:48.290 --> 01:00:52.490
Then this says, show me the
set of all parameters that

01:00:52.490 --> 01:00:55.880
fit my data at least
as well as alpha.

01:00:55.880 --> 01:00:58.110
Everybody got that?

01:00:58.110 --> 01:01:00.200
OK, so it kind of
makes sense, right?

01:01:00.200 --> 01:01:02.180
I mean, it's an
interesting-- and it

01:01:02.180 --> 01:01:04.940
says that these-- if
you're quasiconvex, those

01:01:04.940 --> 01:01:07.970
are convex functions.

01:01:07.970 --> 01:01:09.650
