01:09:20.170 --> 01:09:22.630
But we'll do one example.

01:09:22.630 --> 01:09:25.479
This is quite practical.

01:09:25.479 --> 01:09:28.240
It's the internal
rate of return.

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:29.680
So this is from--

01:09:29.680 --> 01:09:31.990
I don't know if you could--
accounting or something.

01:09:31.990 --> 01:09:33.490
OK, so here it is.

01:09:33.490 --> 01:09:35.020
So I have a cash flow.

01:09:35.020 --> 01:09:37.960
And it's given by a vector,
which is n plus 1 long.

01:09:37.960 --> 01:09:41.260
And the entries are labeled
like x1, x0, up to xn.

01:09:41.260 --> 01:09:44.890
So that's a cash flow
over n plus 1 periods.

01:09:44.890 --> 01:09:48.189
I mean, it depends. x0
might be like right now.

01:09:48.189 --> 01:09:50.080
So, for example, if
you bought a bond--

01:09:50.080 --> 01:09:51.130
let's just say.

01:09:51.130 --> 01:09:54.400
If you bought a bond,
x0 might be negative.

01:09:54.400 --> 01:09:56.720
It's how much you
pay for the bond.

01:09:56.720 --> 01:10:02.210
Then x1 might be the coupon
payment you get for that bond.

01:10:02.210 --> 01:10:04.850
x2 is the coupon
payment in period 2.

01:10:04.850 --> 01:10:06.560
And then xn might
be the maturity.

01:10:06.560 --> 01:10:10.370
And you get both back the coupon
payment and the face value.

01:10:10.370 --> 01:10:13.670
By the way, you don't have
to know what a bond is.

01:10:13.670 --> 01:10:14.870
You don't need to know this.

01:10:14.870 --> 01:10:16.250
But it's not bad.

01:10:16.250 --> 01:10:19.280
It's just cultural-- I don't
know-- enrichment or something.

01:10:19.280 --> 01:10:21.530
Everybody got that?

01:10:21.530 --> 01:10:26.600
I mean, this could be an
investment made by a VC firm

01:10:26.600 --> 01:10:29.810
in a startup.

01:10:29.810 --> 01:10:35.570
So they wire transfer $12
million into a bank account.

01:10:35.570 --> 01:10:36.950
That's x0.

01:10:36.950 --> 01:10:41.630
Then x1 is probably
0 for a startup.

01:10:41.630 --> 01:10:46.070
And then maybe sometime
later, somebody acquires them.

01:10:46.070 --> 01:10:47.840
And then one of the
x's is really big.

01:10:47.840 --> 01:10:49.280
Everybody got this?

01:10:49.280 --> 01:10:52.580
OK, so that's a cash flow.

01:10:52.580 --> 01:10:53.990
And we're going to assume--

01:10:53.990 --> 01:10:55.070
this is just to make it--

01:10:55.070 --> 01:10:56.120
to make the definition simple.

01:10:56.120 --> 01:10:56.655
That's fine.

01:10:56.655 --> 01:10:58.530
We're going to assume
that x0 is less than 0.

01:10:58.530 --> 01:11:04.410
That means that the first point
in that, the first cash flow

01:11:04.410 --> 01:11:08.083
is something we
pay, we invest in.

01:11:08.083 --> 01:11:09.750
Because it's a negative
cash flow to us.

01:11:09.750 --> 01:11:11.880
It means we're investing at 0.

01:11:11.880 --> 01:11:13.920
Then we're also
going to assume--

01:11:13.920 --> 01:11:17.340
we don't assume that the signs
of x1 through xn are positive.

01:11:17.340 --> 01:11:18.810
We may pay more in.

01:11:18.810 --> 01:11:20.250
They can come back
to us and say,

01:11:20.250 --> 01:11:22.490
sorry, we need a bridge
loan or whatever.

01:11:22.490 --> 01:11:23.490
Or we don't-- that's it.

01:11:23.490 --> 01:11:25.020
Or we can't meet payroll.

01:11:25.020 --> 01:11:26.040
So we need more money.

01:11:26.040 --> 01:11:27.840
That's fine.

01:11:27.840 --> 01:11:31.230
But we will assume that simply
the sum of all the things

01:11:31.230 --> 01:11:31.830
is positive.

01:11:31.830 --> 01:11:33.442
And what that means--

01:11:33.442 --> 01:11:34.900
and this is just
to make it simple.

01:11:34.900 --> 01:11:36.300
It works in other cases too.

01:11:36.300 --> 01:11:40.330
What that means is that the
total of all the cash payments

01:11:40.330 --> 01:11:40.890
is--

01:11:40.890 --> 01:11:43.450
it was not at least a disaster.

01:11:43.450 --> 01:11:46.170
That's what it means.

01:11:46.170 --> 01:11:49.350
So that's what it says.

01:11:49.350 --> 01:11:52.020
So people talk about the
present value of the cash flow.

01:11:52.020 --> 01:11:55.020
And that's going to be with
an interest rate R. It says,

01:11:55.020 --> 01:11:57.030
you simply multiply--

01:11:57.030 --> 01:12:03.660
so what you do is you say that
a payment to me at time I,

01:12:03.660 --> 01:12:08.280
at period I, is going to be
worth 1 plus R to the minus I.

01:12:08.280 --> 01:12:15.630
So if R is 5% and it's 10 years,
it means that it's worth to me

01:12:15.630 --> 01:12:19.350
only 1 over 1.05 to the 10.

01:12:19.350 --> 01:12:23.310
I don't know what that
is, 1.8, 1 over 1.8.

01:12:23.310 --> 01:12:25.000
Let's say it's 0.4.

01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:27.060
I have no idea what it is, 0.5.

01:12:27.060 --> 01:12:29.370
I'm making that up.

01:12:29.370 --> 01:12:32.730
So these are the
discounted cash flows.

01:12:32.730 --> 01:12:34.672
And this is called
the net present value.

01:12:34.672 --> 01:12:36.630
And these are just
completely standard concepts

01:12:36.630 --> 01:12:40.282
in basic accounting.

01:12:40.282 --> 01:12:41.490
OK, that's the present value.

01:12:41.490 --> 01:12:44.760
And it's a function of the
vector x and the interest

01:12:44.760 --> 01:12:46.825
rate R.

01:12:46.825 --> 01:12:49.200
And now, I can tell you what
the Internal Rate of Return.

01:12:49.200 --> 01:12:50.460
It's called the IRR.

01:12:50.460 --> 01:12:53.850
It's the smallest
interest rate for which

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:57.630
the net present value is 0.

01:12:57.630 --> 01:12:59.850
And I think the
argument about it,

01:12:59.850 --> 01:13:01.650
if you're teaching
MBAs or something,

01:13:01.650 --> 01:13:05.723
is the idea is you would be
ambivalent, if I walk up to you

01:13:05.723 --> 01:13:07.140
and I say, hey, I
got a great deal

01:13:07.140 --> 01:13:09.178
for you invest in this thing.

01:13:09.178 --> 01:13:11.220
And then we work out the
internal rate of return.

01:13:11.220 --> 01:13:13.653
And it turns out you
could get something else.

01:13:13.653 --> 01:13:15.570
You could get a-- you
could get a bank account

01:13:15.570 --> 01:13:19.200
or something like that with
the same interest rate.

01:13:19.200 --> 01:13:21.720
Then you'd say, why should I
invest in your stupid thing?

01:13:21.720 --> 01:13:23.880
I'm going to take it to
the bank or something like.

01:13:23.880 --> 01:13:26.370
That's the idea.

01:13:26.370 --> 01:13:27.750
Everybody got this?

01:13:27.750 --> 01:13:31.273
And this is-- by the way,
this is not part of the class.

01:13:31.273 --> 01:13:32.440
You don't need to know this.

01:13:32.440 --> 01:13:34.630
But it doesn't hurt to know it.

01:13:34.630 --> 01:13:36.210
This is not bad.

01:13:36.210 --> 01:13:38.255
OK, all right.

01:13:38.255 --> 01:13:39.630
So it turns out
the internal rate

01:13:39.630 --> 01:13:43.410
of return, which is
actually complicated,

01:13:43.410 --> 01:13:45.780
because he actually--
this thing here is

01:13:45.780 --> 01:13:50.040
a polynomial in 1 over 1 plus
R. Yeah, it is a polynomial.

01:13:50.040 --> 01:13:52.870
So this actually requires
solving a polynomial equation.

01:13:52.870 --> 01:13:53.888
So hello.

01:13:53.888 --> 01:13:55.680
If there's more than
one fifth-- if there's

01:13:55.680 --> 01:13:58.500
more than five
periods, then there's

01:13:58.500 --> 01:14:00.100
not an analytical
formula for it.

01:14:00.100 --> 01:14:01.560
But who cares?

01:14:01.560 --> 01:14:03.570
Everybody following?

01:14:03.570 --> 01:14:06.090
So it's complicated.

01:14:06.090 --> 01:14:08.970
So this is the internal
rate of return.

01:14:08.970 --> 01:14:12.520
And it turns out
it's quasiconcave,

01:14:12.520 --> 01:14:15.290
which is just weird.

01:14:15.290 --> 01:14:17.940
I doubt anyone in
accounting would know this.

01:14:17.940 --> 01:14:21.250
In fact, I'm virtually
certain of that fact,

01:14:21.250 --> 01:14:24.165
I have friends in accounting.

01:14:24.165 --> 01:14:25.290
And I'm not insulting them.

01:14:25.290 --> 01:14:27.510
I'm just saying it's
not how they think.

01:14:27.510 --> 01:14:28.980
So it's fine.

01:14:28.980 --> 01:14:30.330
It's quasiconcave.

01:14:30.330 --> 01:14:33.570
So what does it mean to show
something's quasiconcave?

01:14:33.570 --> 01:14:35.970
What you need to do
is you need to show

01:14:35.970 --> 01:14:39.090
that the super level sets--

01:14:39.090 --> 01:14:41.640
quasiconvex means the
sublevel sets are convex.

01:14:41.640 --> 01:14:47.280
Quasiconcave means the
super level sets are convex.

01:14:47.280 --> 01:14:50.110
And so let's look at that.

01:14:50.110 --> 01:14:53.820
So let's look at the
set of all cash flows

01:14:53.820 --> 01:14:56.620
here that satisfy the
internal rate of return

01:14:56.620 --> 01:15:00.070
is bigger than, let's say, 5%.

01:15:00.070 --> 01:15:02.260
That's what we're
going to look at.

01:15:02.260 --> 01:15:05.020
So what is that?

01:15:05.020 --> 01:15:08.020
If your internal rate of return
is bigger than 5-- bigger than

01:15:08.020 --> 01:15:11.740
or equal to 5%, it means
that between an interest

01:15:11.740 --> 01:15:15.220
rate of 0% and 5%, your
net present value was still

01:15:15.220 --> 01:15:17.640
positive.

01:15:17.640 --> 01:15:21.870
Because this says that your net
present value with an interest

01:15:21.870 --> 01:15:24.060
rate of 0 is positive.

01:15:24.060 --> 01:15:25.390
That's what it says.

01:15:25.390 --> 01:15:27.420
It says that
there's no interest.

01:15:27.420 --> 01:15:29.280
It's low interest time.

01:15:29.280 --> 01:15:31.500
So just add up the numbers.

01:15:31.500 --> 01:15:33.090
And that says it's positive.

01:15:33.090 --> 01:15:38.070
So the project looks good if the
current interest rates are 0.

01:15:38.070 --> 01:15:41.010
As you increase it,
what you can figure out

01:15:41.010 --> 01:15:42.820
is actually this
thing decreases.

01:15:42.820 --> 01:15:44.820
And, of course, at some
point it goes through 0.

01:15:44.820 --> 01:15:47.278
When it goes through 0, that's
the internal rate of return.

01:15:49.830 --> 01:15:56.980
So what is this as
a function of x?

01:15:56.980 --> 01:15:58.245
What is that?

01:16:01.220 --> 01:16:02.440
So--

01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:03.460
Just a linear function.

01:16:03.460 --> 01:16:05.440
Oh, that's a linear function.

01:16:05.440 --> 01:16:08.590
But then this says the linear
function is bigger than 0.

01:16:08.590 --> 01:16:10.310
So what is that?

01:16:10.310 --> 01:16:10.810
Half-space.

01:16:10.810 --> 01:16:11.660
It's a half-space.

01:16:11.660 --> 01:16:13.390
It's an open half-space,
you would say,

01:16:13.390 --> 01:16:14.980
because it doesn't have the
bigger than or equal to.

01:16:14.980 --> 01:16:15.897
But it doesn't matter.

01:16:15.897 --> 01:16:18.280
So it's an open half-space.

01:16:18.280 --> 01:16:22.330
Oh, and an open
half-space is convex.

01:16:22.330 --> 01:16:27.000
And for the IRR to
be bigger than R,

01:16:27.000 --> 01:16:28.680
this thing has to hold--

01:16:28.680 --> 01:16:31.470
you have to be in a
family, in an intersection

01:16:31.470 --> 01:16:36.230
of a set of open
half-spaces that's convex.

01:16:36.230 --> 01:16:38.240
Everybody follow this?

01:16:38.240 --> 01:16:39.230
Yes.

01:16:39.230 --> 01:16:41.420
Is that like, you're doing
an unaccountably infinite

01:16:41.420 --> 01:16:42.260
intersection?

01:16:42.260 --> 01:16:44.090
No, that's the cool thing.

01:16:44.090 --> 01:16:45.860
Yeah, it's a great question.

01:16:45.860 --> 01:16:51.710
Max and min, they
generalize to sup and inf.

01:16:51.710 --> 01:16:54.620
And, in fact, they can
be over infinite sets.

01:16:54.620 --> 01:16:57.500
And we saw a whole
bunch of examples,

01:16:57.500 --> 01:16:58.460
where that's the case.

01:16:58.460 --> 01:16:59.790
This is another one.

01:16:59.790 --> 01:17:00.795
So yeah.

01:17:00.795 --> 01:17:02.420
So that's a very good
thing to remember

01:17:02.420 --> 01:17:05.840
is these rules
about max and min,

01:17:05.840 --> 01:17:08.900
they can be on infinite sets.

01:17:08.900 --> 01:17:11.510
So, OK.

01:17:11.510 --> 01:17:12.380
Everybody got this?

01:17:12.380 --> 01:17:13.190
Yeah.

01:17:13.190 --> 01:17:19.460
So when we talk about concavity,
not convexity for this one,

01:17:19.460 --> 01:17:21.680
super level implies concavity.

01:17:21.680 --> 01:17:24.890
But the intersection of open
half-spaces, open half-space

01:17:24.890 --> 01:17:26.480
are also concave?

01:17:26.480 --> 01:17:27.320
No.

01:17:27.320 --> 01:17:27.980
Oh, OK, no.

01:17:27.980 --> 01:17:31.190
So open half-space are convex.

01:17:31.190 --> 01:17:33.230
Be careful, we don't have--

01:17:33.230 --> 01:17:35.720
there's no such thing
as a concave set.

01:17:35.720 --> 01:17:37.220
Or at least I don't
know what it is.

01:17:37.220 --> 01:17:38.595
I don't think it
makes any sense.

01:17:38.595 --> 01:17:39.750
So we don't have concave.

01:17:39.750 --> 01:17:40.880
That's weird.

01:17:40.880 --> 01:17:42.950
There should be something.

01:17:42.950 --> 01:17:45.350
But I don't know what
a concave set is.

01:17:45.350 --> 01:17:46.310
Oh, you have convex--

01:17:46.310 --> 01:17:49.550
you have concave mirrors
and concave lenses.

01:17:49.550 --> 01:17:52.190
But I don't know why we
don't have concave sets.

01:17:52.190 --> 01:17:54.140
Anyway, we don't.

01:17:54.140 --> 01:17:57.350
So here, what you want to show--
to show this quasiconcave,

01:17:57.350 --> 01:18:01.600
you want to show that the
super level sets are convex.

01:18:01.600 --> 01:18:04.054
So yeah.

01:18:04.054 --> 01:18:08.650
[INAUDIBLE] the summation of 1
plus R, that's open half-space,

01:18:08.650 --> 01:18:11.320
but where does the
intersection come into that?

01:18:11.320 --> 01:18:14.200
Because here's
what has to happen.

01:18:14.200 --> 01:18:17.980
For x to be a cash flow with
an internal rate of return

01:18:17.980 --> 01:18:21.880
exceeding R, then
this inequality

01:18:21.880 --> 01:18:26.590
has to hold for all real
numbers between 0 and capital R.

01:18:26.590 --> 01:18:28.750
And so basically, you're
in the intersection

01:18:28.750 --> 01:18:30.280
of all of those half-spaces.

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:33.070
For any fixed number R,
you get a half-space.

01:18:33.070 --> 01:18:36.430
But you got to satisfy that for
all numbers between 0 and R.

01:18:36.430 --> 01:18:38.530
And that's an intersection.

01:18:38.530 --> 01:18:40.640
Does that make sense?

01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:43.570
Cool, OK.

01:18:43.570 --> 01:18:45.260
So I'll just say
a few more things,

01:18:45.260 --> 01:18:49.400
and then we'll
continue next time.

01:18:49.400 --> 01:18:51.820
So for quasiconvex
functions, you

01:18:51.820 --> 01:18:53.260
have a modified
Jensen inequality.

01:18:53.260 --> 01:18:54.593
It's actually quite interesting.

01:18:54.593 --> 01:18:58.750
Jensen's inequality says that
f of a mixture is less than

01:18:58.750 --> 01:19:00.100
the mixture of f's.

01:19:00.100 --> 01:19:02.510
That's Jensen's inequality
for a convex function.

01:19:02.510 --> 01:19:05.930
For quasiconvex, this gets
replaced with the max.

01:19:05.930 --> 01:19:07.452
And the picture is--

01:19:07.452 --> 01:19:08.660
I don't have a picture there.

01:19:08.660 --> 01:19:09.020
I should.

01:19:09.020 --> 01:19:10.062
I don't know why I don't.

01:19:10.062 --> 01:19:11.880
OK, so here's what
it looks like.

01:19:11.880 --> 01:19:14.390
Here's a quasiconvex function.

01:19:14.390 --> 01:19:16.640
And then here's x.

01:19:16.640 --> 01:19:19.100
And here is-- here's x.

01:19:19.100 --> 01:19:20.840
And here's y.

01:19:20.840 --> 01:19:23.390
Convexity says that if I draw
a straight line between here

01:19:23.390 --> 01:19:25.610
and here, the graph
lies below it.

01:19:25.610 --> 01:19:27.980
But that's clearly
false for this example.

01:19:27.980 --> 01:19:31.442
And sorry to pick a color,
which is unreadable.

01:19:31.442 --> 01:19:32.150
Sorry about that.

01:19:32.150 --> 01:19:33.170
So, OK.

01:19:33.170 --> 01:19:37.550
So this thing says, for
quasiconvexity, here's

01:19:37.550 --> 01:19:38.540
what you want to do.

01:19:38.540 --> 01:19:40.800
You take the max of the two.

01:19:40.800 --> 01:19:43.130
And so it basically
says the graph

01:19:43.130 --> 01:19:45.950
has to lie between
under that thing.

01:19:45.950 --> 01:19:47.150
And it does.

01:19:47.150 --> 01:19:48.080
So it's cool.

01:19:48.080 --> 01:19:49.430
So that's quasiconvex.

01:19:49.430 --> 01:19:52.040
OK, so we'll quit today.

01:19:52.040 --> 01:19:54.380
I have to say, we
are very close.

01:19:54.380 --> 01:19:55.790
We're about 10 to 15--

01:19:55.790 --> 01:20:00.260
10 to 15 minutes of lecture
through this long slog

01:20:00.260 --> 01:20:01.930
of all the math.

01:20:01.930 --> 01:20:05.200
But the sun will come out, I
would say, about 20 minutes

01:20:05.200 --> 01:20:09.120
into next Tuesday's lecture.

